From 0d9a23200284824849b2f6008d9544675f1f40b3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cruz <cruz@tbu.net>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 18:26:21 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 013/206] feat(shorebird): load_obfuscation_map -- reuse a base
 build's renames

Reproduces Shorebird's --load-obfuscation-map, so a patch build renames the
identifiers it shares with its base identically. gen_snapshot --help is now
byte-identical to theirs, as is the VM flag description.

It has to be a VM flag, not an embedder API: Dart_CreateIsolateGroupFromKernel
loads dart:core and already renames while loading, so a map installed from the
embedder arrives after the class members were registered under a fresh
counter's renames, and later lookups fail (Lookup failed: _normalized in
dart:core in @constructors in _BigIntImpl). Only compiling a program different
from the map's origin exposes it -- a self-round-trip passes because the fresh
renames coincide. So the map is seeded lazily from the Obfuscator constructor,
which the first rename triggers, i.e. before kernel translation.

SerializeMap never writes the counter at kSavedStateNameIndex, so a loader has
to recover it. We resume past the largest rename in the map, ordering by
NextName's numeral system (little-endian digits a..z A..Z). Shorebird resumes
short of that and re-issues 44 names already taken by library URIs, breaking
the injectivity their own fresh builds hold; we decline to reproduce that.

Verified against their binary on a base/extended fixture pair:
  - round-trip base -> load -> save: dict-equal
  - all 4568 base renames preserved when compiling a changed program
  - duplicate renames: ours 0, theirs 8
  - seeded with their map, our output equals theirs exactly (4568/4568) on the
    same program; on a changed one only the 12 new-symbol entries differ

